{
  "dom_id": "step-q71049",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $U(-3,-2,0)$ and $V(1,0,-1)$, and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨-3,-2,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{UV} \\\\ r & =⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨4,2,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
